{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nThe current tickets branch stores ticket event dirs flat at `tickets/<id>`, making the root tree about 270 KB and causing every single-ticket commit to re-emit that large root tree. W2 changes only the on-disk layout to reduce per-commit tree churn. S1 history reclaim, W1 batching, and any live shared-store cutover are out of scope.\n\n## Scope\n\nImplement a fan-out layout `tickets/<first-two-hex>/<id>` for canonical ticket ids. New code must read legacy flat and sharded stores during the expand phase, preserve all ids/aliases/events, and provide an idempotent ensure-registry migration. Do not run this migration against the live shared `.tickets-tracker`; fixture/throwaway stores only.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] New ticket writes land at `tickets/<first-two-hex>/<id>`; `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout.py` proves a fixture single-ticket mutation makes the top-level tree bytes at least 80% smaller than an equivalent flat layout.\n- [ ] Alias, short-id, and full-id resolution are unchanged for flat, mixed, and migrated stores; `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout_heldout.py` plus existing resolver selectors prove this.\n- [ ] Event replay/listing is unchanged for flat, mixed, and migrated stores; targeted reducer/list tests prove the same states are returned.\n- [ ] The migration maps every flat ticket dir to its deterministic shard, leaves no flat ticket dirs behind, preserves identities/event bytes, and a second run makes no commit; `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout.py` proves this.\n- [ ] Migration collisions are fatal and atomic: two source paths mapping to the same sharded target abort before any partial commit/move; `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout.py` proves this.\n- [ ] Merge-as-union reconvergence still preserves both sides on a two-clone sharded fixture; `tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout_heldout.py` proves both commits remain reachable.\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n1. Add `rebar._store.ticket_layout` as the single layout owner: shard/relpath derivation, existing-dir resolution, deterministic ticket iteration, shard-container recognition, pinned-tree path parsing, shard-container mkdir, and `migrate_flat_ticket_dirs_unit`.\n2. Writers create the shard container with `exist_ok=True` under the write lock before the atomic `os.rename(staging_dir, ticket_dir)`, preserving ticket+first-event atomic publication while an empty shard container remains benign.\n3. Keep resolver outputs separator-free. `_ids.py` continues returning ids/names, while exact lookup and prefix/alias scans build the same sorted id index from flat and sharded dirs via the layout helper.\n4. Route write/read path composition through the helper. Required inventory: `_store/{staging,gitutil,store_usability,compat,ensures,env_identity,hlc}.py`; `reducer/{_api,_inbound}.py`; `_ids.py`; `_engine_support/{field_reads,lookups,reads,validate,next_batch,commit_impact}.py`; graph `{_cache,_graph,_hierarchy,_links,_status}.py`; commands `{claim,transition,txn,delete,compact,compact_plan,compact_rebuild,compact_recovery,compact_trigger,compact_txn,close_precheck,close_disposition,fsck_scan,fsck_repair,fsck_tracker_health,fsck_recover,fsck_authorship,fsck_restore,identity,session_log,tracker_maintenance,trusted_env_cmd,verify_authorship}.py`; sidecar/bot readers `{attest/authorship,signing,metrics/{blame,event_metrics,sidecar_metrics,bug_trends},_snapshot/{ticket_view,ticket_objects},llm/{completion_sidecar,code_review/sidecar,overlap/queue,overlap/digest_sidecar,enrich_drain,plan_review/claimability,plan_review/relation_snapshot,plan_review/sidecar,epic_bug_screen,workflow/recorder},_io/{export_ndjson,import_ndjson},_engine/rebar_reconciler/{transition_replay,local_label_intent,impossible_links}}.py`.\n6. Register `ticket-layout-shards` in `_store.ensures`. It runs under the existing write lock, precomputes all moves before mutation, refuses target collisions as a fatal/permanent operator-repair error, stages old+new paths, and commits one migration commit only when drift exists.\n6. Register `sharded-ticket-layout` in `compat.KNOWN_CAPABILITIES` before any store stamps it. The migration appends that capability so older v1.0+ binaries fail closed on migrated stores; current binaries keep reading them.\n7. Make fsck/store-health depth-aware: valid shard containers are not `FOREIGN_STORE_PATH`, while unrelated top-level dirs still are.\n8. Update pinned snapshot readers to parse both `<id>/<event>` and `<shard>/<id>/<event>` through the layout helper.\n9. Preserve expand-phase compatibility: before migration, readers handle both layouts and existing flat tickets may still be updated in place; after migration, all tickets resolve to shards. Live cutover is deferred to the operator.\n\n10. The migration holds the existing store write lock for one bounded local filesystem rename batch and one local git commit; no network push happens inside the lock. The live 6,008-ticket cutover is still operator-scheduled so they can choose a maintenance window.\n\n## Surface-Parity Census\n\n- CLI: in scope via subprocess create/comment/show fixture tests.\n- MCP/API: out of scope for live MCP because the server may run older code; accepted because tools delegate to the same library/core paths.\n- Library: in scope via `import rebar` create/show/reduce tests.\n- Automated callers: in scope through inventory updates and targeted unit selectors; no live Jira/LLM calls.\n- Dual-direction: in scope for local read/write and import/export; outbound Jira field semantics unchanged.\n\n## Testing / Verification\n\nRun targeted RED/GREEN selectors: `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout.py tests/unit/test_store_sharded_layout_heldout.py -q`, existing resolver/reducer selectors, and reconvergence/store path selectors. Final preflight: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `make test` with the worktree `.venv/bin` first on `PATH`.\n\n## Deployment / Rollback\n\nThis change ships mechanism only. Applying it to the live shared store is an operator decision and is not performed here. After an operator migrates a store, rollback is a tickets-branch revert or approved tracker-maintenance action, not an agent force/bypass.\n\n## Portability\n\nIn-process layout/migration only; no CI-provider dependency."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788240548914594001,"uuid":"bec278b0-2d6f-4ff9-97b4-8516162b174e"}